OpenMiles: an IMA ADPCM sample's loop block is in bytes of its blocks

AIL_set_sample_loop_block takes byte offsets into the sample's own data,
so for IMA ADPCM they are offsets into the compressed blocks; Miles 6.0i
loops at the start of the block each offset falls in. NOLF's engine sets a
sound's loop from its WAV cue region this way (clientsoundinst.cpp), as a
fraction of the data size. OpenMiles v0.4.0 divided the offsets by the
decoded frame size instead, which for 4-bit ADPCM decoded to floats puts
the loop at about an eighth of where it belongs. The crane outside the
warehouse in M05S03 (LIFT3.wav, cue region frames 7148..18374, given as
bytes 3709..9535 of 512-byte blocks) played its first 108 ms and then
looped a 66 ms stretch of it for as long as the crane moved, a loud
rattling drill (nolfsrc issue #31); recorded in-game, the retail DLL
plays to 830 ms and loops back to 323 ms (frames 18306 and 7119), and so
does OpenMiles now. The frame-to-byte direction (a loop block end of -2,
"keep") converts back the same way. Applied by build/ext.sh.

